---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/heygen-editor.png';
import editorBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-editor-before.png';
import layout1 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-1.png';
import layout2 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-2.png';
import layout3 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-3.png';
import layout4 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-4.png';
import timelineBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-timeline-before.png';
import timelineAfter from '../../assets/img/heygen-timeline-after.png';
import playbarFloating from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-floating.png';
import playbarNone from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-none.png';
import playbarRow from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-row.png';
import errorBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-error-before.png';
import errorAfter from '../../assets/img/heygen-error-after.png';
import iosHow from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-how.png';
import iosTopic from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-topic.png';
import iosConsent from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-consent.png';
import layouts from '../../assets/img/heygen-layouts.png';
import scriptBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-script-before.png';
import scriptAfter from '../../assets/img/heygen-script-after.png';
import menusBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-menus-before.png';
import menusAfter from '../../assets/img/heygen-menus-after.png';
import iterations from '../../assets/img/heygen-iterations.png';
import flows from '../../assets/img/heygen-flows.png';
import iosScope from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-scope.png';

// Every frame is a region of one of the Webflow case page's own images, cut so that the deck's
// annotations stay out of the picture: the image shows, the label names, the words argue. The
// play bar's three states are the band between canvas and timeline, stacked at the row's width —
// the largest figure after the hero, because it is the decision the page would show first.
const eaton = {
  when: '2022.06 – 2022.08', org: 'Eaton Corporation', role: 'UX Design Intern', title: 'RapidLinkX',
  sub: 'An IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems, with parameter editing and error monitoring.',
  to: '/projects/eaton',
};
---
<Case
  title="Heygen — Kyle Xu"
  description="Product Design Intern at Heygen, June–October 2023. The AI video editor re-architected around the script with an updated component library — engagement +32.1%, task completion +24.3% against the flow it replaced — then a 0→1 iOS companion MVP for avatar creation."
  ogImage="/og-heygen.jpg"
  eyebrow="Heygen · Product Design Intern · June – October 2023"
  deck="Script, canvas and timeline reorganised for customers who'd outgrown a one-page editor; then a 0→1 iOS companion in three weeks."
  next={eaton}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">The video editor, <span class="nb">re-architected</span> around the script.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="The Heygen video editor: two script cards in a dark panel on the left, an avatar on a white canvas saying Feel free to reach out, and below them a transport row and a timeline with a ruler and playhead"
    caption="The editor as it shipped: the script in the sidebar, the canvas, and the timeline under its own transport row." />

  <section class="col sec" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>The desktop editor — script panel, timeline, component library — as its only designer; then the iOS companion MVP for avatar creation, a <span class="nb">three-week</span> sprint.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Shipped</dt>
        <dd>The <span class="nb">re-architected</span> editor and its updated component library. The iOS MVP reached a <span class="nb">mid-fidelity</span> prototype and early validation, then was postponed.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>Engagement +32.1% and task completion +24.3%, against the editor flow it replaced.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-hard">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-hard">Why it was hard</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Heygen started with one job, an avatar reading a script, and the editor was built for it: the script box under the canvas, a row of scene cards for a timeline, everything on one page so there was nothing to learn. By 2023 the customers were small studios, individual creators and businesses making <span class="nb">multi-scene</span> videos, and the one page had become the ceiling: no room for a second script, no way to see time.</p>
      <p><span class="nb">Text-to-speech</span> was still the core of the product, so whatever came next had to give the script more room. Product and engineering read the scope differently and I was the only designer on it, so I proposed a <span class="nb">one-week</span> design sprint to get everyone to the same starting layout.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={editorBefore} evidence="Supporting state" narrow
        alt="The old editor's lower half: a script box with one sentence typed in, a voice card for Aria with a speed slider, a Play Scripts button, and beneath them a timeline holding one card and a play button"
        caption="The editor before: one script box, one voice, and a timeline of one card with no ruler." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-script">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-script">Where the script lives</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The timeline needed the bottom of the page, which freed the script's old spot and raised the question of where it should go. I drew four layouts. Two added a <span class="nb">right-hand</span> panel, easy to extend, but it moved the script to the far edge of the screen and made it one panel among several. Two moved it into the left sidebar, as a tab beside Template, Avatar, Text, Element and Asset.</p>
      <p>I chose the plainest: the sidebar carries the script and nothing else moves. It was the least extensible option, and the sheet said so, but an existing user would open the new editor and still know where they were. With a customer base to keep, that mattered more.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="four" evidence="Concept"
        items={[
          { src: layout1, dim: true, alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar on the left, canvas in the middle, script and action panels stacked on the right, timeline along the bottom', name: 'Right panel', note: 'script and actions in a new module' },
          { src: layout2, dim: true, alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar on the left, canvas with an action panel over its lower edge, a script panel on the right, timeline along the bottom', name: 'Right panel', note: 'the script permanent, actions over the canvas' },
          { src: layout3, dim: true, alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar and script together on the left, canvas in the middle, an action panel between canvas and timeline', name: 'Sidebar', note: 'the script in it, an action panel under the canvas' },
          { src: layout4, alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar and script together on the left, canvas in the middle, timeline along the bottom, nothing else', name: 'Sidebar', note: 'the script in it, nothing else moved — chosen' },
        ]}
        caption="The four layouts from the sprint, the script in orange. The last one shipped." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-timeline">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-timeline">A timeline that shows time</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The old timeline showed cards, not time: no ruler, no playhead, and state changes so quiet that people weren't sure what they'd selected. A full <span class="nb">multi-track</span> suite would have been too much for the beginners who were most of the base.</p>
      <p>I kept the three tracks — avatar, script, audio — and gave them a ruler, a playhead, an expand toggle and clearer drag states, under three rules: each panel has a visible boundary; every element is one attribute, with no hidden binding to another; and whatever appears in two panels stays in sync, so a scene selected in the timeline lights up on the canvas. We tested it with agencies and individual creators, and what changed afterwards was mostly text: stronger state cues on the script cards, hotkey hints where a tooltip had been.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" evidence="Shipped"
        items={[
          { src: timelineBefore, alt: 'The old timeline: a play button, then three rows of cards for the avatar, the script and the scenes, with coarse marks at 0, 15, 30 and 45 seconds and no playhead', name: 'Before' },
          { src: timelineAfter, alt: 'The new timeline: an expand toggle, transport buttons, a ruler marked every ten seconds, a playhead reading 00:38:88, and the avatar, scene and script tracks in a fixed order', name: 'After' },
        ]}
        caption="The timeline before and after: a ruler, a playhead with the current time, an expand toggle, and the three tracks in a fixed order." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-playbar">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-playbar">The play bar, twice</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>In the first round I drew a play bar floating over the canvas. It didn't get through — too much on the canvas — so I went the other way and proposed dropping the play bar altogether: the timeline had a playhead now, and one control fewer would read as simpler.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" evidence="Shipped"
        items={[
          { src: playbarFloating, alt: 'The band between canvas and timeline, with a play bar floating over the lower edge of the canvas: play, a progress line, 00:00 of 00:30, zoom', name: 'Floating', note: 'the first round, turned down' },
          { src: playbarNone, alt: 'The same band with no play bar: the canvas ends, and the timeline begins with a lone play button beside its tracks', name: 'None', note: 'my proposal, and the one people kept reaching past' },
          { src: playbarRow, alt: 'The same band with a slim row between canvas and timeline: expand, previous, play, next, and zoom controls', name: 'Row', note: 'what shipped' },
        ]}
        caption="The band between canvas and timeline in the three states the play bar went through. The rest of the editor is blurred in the source; the bar is what changed." />
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>In testing, people kept reaching for it. Preview is the loop this tool runs on — play, watch, fix a line, play again — and the timeline's small transport was too far from their eyes. Engineering had run into trouble of its own with that version. So I put it back, as a slim row between canvas and timeline. The assumption had been mine and it was wrong; the test caught it before anyone built on it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-shipped">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-shipped">How it shipped</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The editor went to engineering as two flow maps, Text to Script and Timeline Editor, with every edge case drawn as a state: an empty script, a generation error, a voice that can't take a pitch setting, a script over the word limit. With a small team, each change had to be checkable on its own, so the maps doubled as the review list. The component library was updated alongside: the script card in each of its states, the voice settings, the context menus with the GPT script writer and Translate, and the timeline's controls.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="pair" evidence="Shipped"
        items={[
          { src: errorBefore, alt: 'The old script card in its error state: the script text unchanged, a small red exclamation icon beside the voice name', name: 'Before' },
          { src: errorAfter, alt: 'The new script card in its error state: the card tinted red, an empty script field, and the line Please type your script text', name: 'After' },
        ]}
        caption="One of the states drawn for the maps, the empty script: before, a red icon in the header and nothing else changes; after, the card turns and the message says what to do." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-ios">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-ios">The iOS companion</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Avatar creation was <span class="nb">desktop-only</span>, and its weakest link was the webcam. A phone's camera is better, so in a <span class="nb">three-week</span> sprint at the end of the internship I led a 0→1 iOS MVP for recording an avatar: scope set with the CPO and product team, 10+ user interviews, six internal evaluation rounds, and a <span class="nb">mid-fidelity</span> prototype of three flows — manage, record, consent.</p>
      <p>The decision inside it was the recording screen: most people freeze without a script, so the teleprompter offers a topic — freestyle, tell us about yourself, a product update — and paces a <span class="nb">thirty-second</span> take, then a <span class="nb">two-minute</span> one. Consent is a spoken sentence recorded in the same session, so the footage carries its own authorisation.</p>
      <p>Early validation was positive. Against Heygen's other initiatives the MVP showed less growth potential, and it was indefinitely postponed. That was the right call. A prototype that works is not, by itself, a case for building the product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="row" evidence="Prototype"
        items={[
          { src: iosHow, alt: 'A phone screen headed How it works: footage plus consent, five minutes, then an instant avatar with your voice clone, and a Continue button' },
          { src: iosTopic, alt: 'A phone screen asking you to choose a topic you feel comfortable talking about for thirty seconds: freestyle, tell us about yourself, your hometown, a product update, your business, a training lesson' },
          { src: iosConsent, alt: 'A phone screen with the camera on the presenter and a consent script beneath: I, Kuanghong Xu, hereby declare that I authorize HeyGen to use the footage of me to build a HeyGen avatar' },
        ]}
        caption="Three screens of the prototype: how it works, the topic picker for a take without a script, and the consent sentence read to camera." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec" aria-label="More states">
    <details class="more">
      <summary>
        <h2>More states</h2>
        <span class="what">The layout sheet with its pros and cons, the script cards and menus before and after, three passes from the sprint week, the two flow maps, and the iOS scope.</span>
        <span class="link">Open</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="grid">
        <Figure src={layouts} evidence="Concept"
          alt="The sprint's layout sheet: four block diagrams over four blurred mock-ups, each with a list of pros and cons; the fourth is marked selected"
          caption="The layout sheet as it was argued in the sprint: each plan with its pros and cons, the fourth marked selected." />
        <Figure set="stack" evidence="Shipped"
          items={[
            { src: scriptBefore, alt: 'Three old script cards: normal, hover with a menu icon, and error with a red icon in the header', name: 'Before' },
            { src: scriptAfter, alt: 'Three new script cards: normal, hover with an outlined field and a play button, and error with the card tinted red and a message to type a script', name: 'After' },
          ]}
          caption="The script card in its normal, hover and error states, before and after." />
        <Figure set="stack" evidence="Shipped"
          items={[
            { src: menusBefore, alt: 'Before: a tip to improve pronunciations, a script context menu with Switch Avatar, Duplicate Script and Delete Script, and a timeline menu with Switch Avatar, Download and Delete', name: 'Before' },
            { src: menusAfter, alt: 'After: hotkey hints for Enter and Shift plus Enter, a script menu with GPT script writer, Translate, Duplicate Script and Delete Script, and a timeline menu with Apply to all avatars, Animation and Delete', name: 'After' },
          ]}
          caption="Guides and the two context menus, before and after: hotkey hints where the pronunciation tip was, and the menus carrying the GPT script writer, Translate and Apply to all avatars." />
        <Figure src={iterations} evidence="Concept"
          alt="Three editor screens from left to right, each with the script in the sidebar and a different timeline"
          caption="Three passes at the editor during the sprint week, from the first sidebar script to the version taken into testing." />
        <Figure src={flows} evidence="Supporting state" half
          alt="Two flow maps of small screens headed Text to Script and Timeline Editor, with each branch labelled"
          caption="The two flow maps handed to engineering, each edge case drawn as a screen." />
        <Figure src={iosScope} evidence="Concept" half
          alt="A scope map in two columns, a two-month scope and a one-year scope, each a tree of features"
          caption="The MVP's scope in two horizons: what ships in two months, and what a year could hold." />
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-outcome">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-outcome">Outcome</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <ul class="nums">
        <li><b>+32.1%</b><span>engagement</span></li>
        <li><b>+24.3%</b><span>task completion</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Both measured against the flow this editor replaced, the <span class="nb">one-page</span> version shown above. The editor shipped with its updated component library; the iOS MVP stayed a prototype.</p>
      <p class="stance">The play bar is the part of this project I'd show first.</p>
      <p>It's the clearest record I have of a test changing my mind, early enough that engineering never built on the wrong assumption. I've tried to work that way since.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Case>
